{"author":{"id":"8022379585b4ab99ca8775db3eb67d33fcb256817114c28e51ccc1e9f55cdec4"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1792310829,"nonce":"drPiQHKpRPzfpwZ/Gf7Y8av1nb8=","title":"Progress on building a debugger / profiler for Rayzor. As of 2026-06-04: P0+P1+P2 LANDED (compiler-side only): backend-instance-tagged JIT ...","message":"**Struct additions:**\n```rust\nfuncid_display_name: BTreeMap\u003cFuncId, String\u003e,\nfuncid_source_loc:   BTreeMap\u003cFuncId, (u32, u32, u32)\u003e, // (file_id, line, col)\nbackend_id:          u64,                                // process-unique\ndumped_funcids:      BTreeMap\u003cFuncId, usize\u003e,            // dedup by (id, addr)\n```\n\n**Population:** at all 3 `function_map.insert` sites (runtime_functions hit, forward-ref link, fresh declare) we now also store the qualified name and `IrFunction.source_location`.\n\n**Dump hook (`maybe_dump_jit_symbols`):** called from 4 places — `finalize()` (multi-module path), `compile_module()` (one-shot path), `compile_and_finalize_function()` (tier-up path), and `impl Drop for CraneliftBackend` (catch-all for owned-and-dropped backends). Each backend gets its own `backend_id` (atomic counter); APPENDs new (backend_id, FuncId, addr) tuples to `/tmp/rayzor_jit_symbols.csv`. Tier-up recompiles to a new address re-emit the row.\n\n**CSV columns:** `backend_id, start_hex, end_hex, size_bytes, func_id, file_id, line, column, qname`\n\n\n**`maybe_dump_file_table()`:** public method that writes `/tmp/rayzor_file_table.csv` (columns: `file_id, path`) when `RAYZOR_DUMP_JIT_MAP=1`. Called from end of `lower_to_tast` so cold-cache compiles populate the file_id ↔ path mapping.\n\n\nLoads the three files (`rayzor_alloc_graph.csv`, `rayzor_jit_symbols.csv`, `rayzor_file_table.csv`), joins them, and emits `/tmp/rayzor_alloc_attributed.csv` with PC frames resolved to `qname (file.hx:line)`. Falls back to `atos -o BIN -arch arm64 0xPC` for PCs in the binary `__TEXT` segment. Prints a per-Haxe-file rollup at the end.\n\n\n```\n$ RAYZOR_DUMP_JIT_MAP=1 ./target/release/rayzor run nue/examples/llama-chat/Main.hx ...\n[jit-map] wrote 80 file paths to /tmp/rayzor_file_table.csv\n[jit-map] backend 1 appended 275 new finalised functions to /tmp/rayzor_jit_symbols.csv\n\n$ head -3 /tmp/rayzor_jit_symbols.csv\nbackend_id,start_hex,end_hex,size_bytes,func_id,file_id,line,column,qname\n1,0x10742c000,0x10742c688,1672,funcid0,79,66,5,\"LocalTempSampler.sample\"\n\n$ awk -F, '$1 == 79' /tmp/rayzor_file_table.csv\n79,\"/Users/amaterasu/Vibranium/rayzor/nue/examples/llama-chat/Main.hx\"\n\n```\n\nTop JIT functions resolved correctly: `nue.transformer.GQAttention.forward`,\n`nue.transformer.TransformerBlock.forward`, `nue.transformer.KVCache.append`,\n`nue.transformer.RMSNorm.forward`, `nue.arch.LlamaModel.forwardIds`, etc.,\neach carrying its `file_id, line, column`. file_id=0 / line=0 entries are\nthe unnamed-stdlib internals where MIR didn't carry source_location.\n\n\n\nSee [bugs-decode-loop-per-step-leak] section \"Diagnostic reproducer\". Paste:\n- ~150 LOC into `runtime/src/lib.rs` after the TRACKED_MAX_SIZE constant\n- `#[global_allocator] static GLOBAL: rayzor_runtime::TrackingAllocator = ...` + `unsafe { rayzor_runtime::ensure_alloc_dump_hooks() };` into `src/main.rs`\n\nThen `RAYZOR_DUMP_ALLOC_AT_EXIT=1 RAYZOR_ALLOC_GRAPH=1 RAYZOR_ALLOC_GRAPH_RATE=1024 ...` produces `/tmp/rayzor_alloc_graph.csv` with sampled PC tuples and `[alloc-stats] allocs=... live=...` summary on atexit.\n\n\nPaste alongside `ensure_alloc_dump_hooks` in runtime/src/lib.rs:\n\n```rust\nstatic CPU_PROFILE_ACTIVE: AtomicU64 = AtomicU64::new(0);\n\nextern \"C\" fn sigprof_handler(_sig: i32) {\n    if IN_GRAPH.with(|g| g.get()) { return; }\n    IN_GRAPH.with(|g| g.set(true));\n    let mut pcs = [0usize; 12];\n    let mut idx = 0;\n    backtrace::trace(|f| {\n        if idx \u003c pcs.len() { pcs[idx] = f.ip() as usize; idx += 1; true } else { false }\n    });\n    let skip = 2.min(idx);\n    let n = (idx - skip).min(6);\n    let mut top = [0usize; 6];\n    top[..n].copy_from_slice(\u0026pcs[skip..skip + n]);\n    use std::hash::{Hash, Hasher};\n    let mut h = std::collections::hash_map::DefaultHasher::new();\n    top.hash(\u0026mut h);\n    // CRITICAL: try_lock — Mutex::lock from a signal handler on a\n    // thread holding the same Mutex deadlocks.\n    if let Ok(mut g) = GRAPH_SITES.try_lock() {\n        if let Some(map) = g.as_mut() {\n            let e = map.entry(h.finish())\n                .or_insert(SiteStat { sampled_count: 0, pcs: top });\n            e.sampled_count += 1;\n        }\n    }\n    IN_GRAPH.with(|g| g.set(false));\n}\n\nunsafe fn install_cpu_profiler(period_us: u64) {\n    extern \"C\" {\n        fn signal(sig: i32, h: extern \"C\" fn(i32)) -\u003e *mut std::ffi::c_void;\n    }\n    signal(27, sigprof_handler); // SIGPROF\n    #[repr(C)] struct Timeval { tv_sec: i64, tv_usec: i32 }\n    #[repr(C)] struct Itimerval { it_interval: Timeval, it_value: Timeval }\n    extern \"C\" {\n        fn setitimer(which: i32, new_value: *const Itimerval, old_value: *mut Itimerval) -\u003e i32;\n    }\n    let sec = (period_us / 1_000_000) as i64;\n    let usec = (period_us % 1_000_000) as i32;\n    let tv = Itimerval {\n        it_interval: Timeval { tv_sec: sec, tv_usec: usec },\n        it_value: Timeval { tv_sec: sec, tv_usec: usec },\n    };\n    setitimer(2, \u0026tv, std::ptr::null_mut()); // ITIMER_PROF\n    CPU_PROFILE_ACTIVE.store(1, MemOrdering::Relaxed);\n}\n```\n\nPlus disarm SIGPROF at top of `rayzor_dump_alloc_graph` (else handler can fire during `std::fs::write` and re-enter):\n```rust\nif CPU_PROFILE_ACTIVE.load(MemOrdering::Relaxed) == 1 {\n    // setitimer(ITIMER_PROF, \u0026zeroed_itimerval, null_mut())\n}\n```\n\nHook into `ensure_alloc_dump_hooks`:\n```rust\nif env::var_os(\"RAYZOR_CPU_PROFILE\") == Some(\"1\".into()) {\n    let period = env::var(\"RAYZOR_CPU_PROFILE_US\").ok()\n        .and_then(|s| s.parse::\u003cu64\u003e().ok()).unwrap_or(1000);\n    { // ensure GRAPH_SITES is initialised\n        let mut g = GRAPH_SITES.lock().unwrap();\n        if g.is_none() { *g = Some(HashMap::new()); }\n    }\n    install_cpu_profiler(period);\n}\n```\n\nThen `RAYZOR_CPU_PROFILE=1 RAYZOR_CPU_PROFILE_US=1000 rayzor run ...` samples PCs every 1ms during execution. Output goes to `/tmp/rayzor_alloc_graph.csv` (same CSV — the resolver doesn't care which signal source filled it). Set rate=0 in the CSV header so the resolver uses raw sample counts.\n\n\n**Commit `c78f34c`** fixes the no-JIT-attribution problem with two changes:\n\n1. **compiler:** `preserve_frame_pointers = \"true\"` on Cranelift settings (default OFF). Every JIT function emits standard `stp fp, lr` prologue + `mov fp, sp`. ~1-2% wall overhead in tight kernels.\n2. **runtime:** manual aarch64 frame-pointer chain walker in the SIGPROF handler. Reads PC + FP from `libc::ucontext_t-\u003euc_mcontext-\u003e__ss` via SA_SIGINFO, then chases the canonical `[saved_fp, saved_lr]` pair. Bypasses libunwind entirely.\n\n**Verified:** SIGPROF resolver on 27-token nue/llama-chat now produces:\n```\nframe attribution: {'jit': 42, 'atos': 607, 'unknown': 71}\n164  LocalTempSampler.sample (Main.hx:66)\n20   GGUFTokenizer.hx (aggregate)\n```\n\n\n**Commit `89ad22f`** + **`4ab2851`**:\n\nEnv-var route (no Haxe changes needed):\n```bash\ntools/profile_run.sh nue/examples/llama-chat/Main.hx -- \u003cgguf\u003e \"prompt\" 30 4096 0.01\n```\n\nFFI route (place explicit start/stop around a region):\n```haxe\n@:native(\"rayzor_profile_start\") extern function startProfile():Void;\n@:native(\"rayzor_profile_stop\")  extern function stopProfile():Void;\n\nstartProfile();\nloop.generate(model, sampler, tokenizer, prompt, onToken);\nstopProfile();\n```\n\nThe runtime exports `rayzor_profile_start` / `rayzor_profile_stop`\nwith idempotent semantics (double-start, double-stop are no-ops). The\nGRAPH_SITES accumulator persists across start/stop windows so you can\nprofile multiple distinct regions in one run and the resolver shows\nthe union.\n\nVerified on 27-token nue/llama-chat with DELAY_MS=2000:\n\n```\n=== alloc volume by Haxe file ===\n  812  Main.hx                   (LocalTempSampler.sample dominates)\n  110  GGUFTokenizer.hx          (build()  ← cold-cache reload tail)\n   70  GGUFReader.hx\n   52  GQAttention.hx            (forward)\n   43  Linear.hx                 (forward)\n```\n\n`GGUFTokenizer.hx` + `GGUFReader.hx` still showing means DELAY_MS=2000\nisn't enough for full cold-cache loading; bump to 5000 for clean\ndecode-only data. `LocalTempSampler.sample` dominates because the\nattribution surfaces the **topmost JIT frame on the call stack**, and\nLocalTempSampler.sample is the leaf of the Haxe call graph that\ndelegates into Rust runtime (argmax / softmax kernel).\n\n\nEarlier suspicion that beadie_jit's `Box::leak`'d backends never dump was **refuted** by `fcf7ea6`'s audit print: every CraneliftBackend reaching the dump path is logged with its function count. In nue/llama-chat short prompts:\n\n```\n[jit-map] backend 1 appended 275 new finalised functions to /tmp/rayzor_jit_symbols.csv\n[jit-map] backend 0 reached dump with 0 defined functions (skip)\n```\n\nOnly 2 backends exist; backend 0 is the original `baseline_backend` from `TieredBackend::new` (empty, never compiles), backend 1 is the fresh runtime backend that handles everything. beadie_jit isn't exercised by short canonical prompts. Coverage is complete for the workloads tested.\n\n\n| Feature | Status |\n|---|---|\n| PC → Haxe qname | ✅ via JIT symbol map |\n| PC → Haxe file:line (per-function) | ✅ via funcid_source_loc + file_table |\n| **PC → Haxe file:line (per-instruction)** | ❌ requires `set_srcloc` per `IrInstruction` in cranelift_backend lowering, plus dumping Cranelift's resulting srcloc table |\n| **Variable inspection** | ❌ requires DWARF-like location records keyed by IrId, ~600 LOC |\n| **Breakpoint / step** | ❌ requires `__jit_debug_register_code` protocol + LLDB integration, ~500 LOC |\n| Worker-thread coverage | ⚠️ partial; needs registry-based dump |\n| Stack traces with Haxe names | ✅ achievable now using current map + `backtrace` crate inside runtime |\n| **CPU profile attribution** | ⚠️ infra works (SIGPROF + resolver); needs gap-1 to fully cover JIT |\n\n\nTwo complementary commits land this session's work:\n\n**Commit A (durable):** `compiler/src/codegen/cranelift_backend.rs` + `compiler/src/compilation.rs` + `tools/resolve_alloc_graph.py`. Zero runtime overhead when `RAYZOR_DUMP_JIT_MAP` unset. Enables stack-trace-with-Haxe-symbols infrastructure and prepares for runtime profilers.\n\n**Commit B (opt-in tooling):** the runtime TrackingAllocator + SIGPROF profiler recipes here, gated behind a feature flag like `--features profile-runtime` so the global_allocator + per-alloc atomic ops only ship to profiler builds.\n\nAfter both land, the remaining ~3-day MVP debugger work is per-instruction `SourceLoc` plumbing (P3, see roadmap) + LLDB integration via `__jit_debug_register_code`.\n\n\n- [[bugs-decode-loop-per-step-leak]] — first consumer; per-step delta investigation is unblocked by the JIT-attributed alloc graph\n- [[project-optimization-roadmap]] — Tier 1 #1 (decode leak), #4 (Q6_K 2×2 tile), #6 (Cranelift fast-math) all benefit from CPU-attributed profiling once worker-thread gap closed\n\n---\nArchived from Claude memory `project_debugger_feasibility` on 2026-10-18. A record of past work; open items in it may have their own issues.","files":null}]}